  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1203 AM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA weak shortwave will move across the region today with warming \ntemperatures, breezy conditions along the Rocky Mountain Front, and \nscattered showers in far southwestern Montana expected. \n\nThe upper-level flow becomes more zonal Tuesday into Wednesday with \ntwo rounds of strong winds expected to move over the northern half \nof the state. The first and stronger wave of winds is expected on \nTuesday with widespread gusts over 30 mph across the northern \nplains, gusts up to 50 mph closer to the Rocky Mountain Front, and \ngusts up to 75 mph possible along the portion of the Northern \nRockies highlighted in the High Wind Watch. The second wave of winds \non Wednesday will be much weaker comparatively with wind gusts \ngenerally staying in the 25 to 35 mph range across much of Central \nand North-Central Montana. The moisture plume associated with this \nzonal flow reaches the Continental Divide in the early morning hours \non Wednesday with a chance for light rain along the Northern Rockies \nthrough the mid-afternoon hours. A few showers may develop across \nNorth-Central Montana, particularly along the Hi-Line, but rainfall \namounts are expected to remain light.\n\nWeak upper-level ridging briefly builds on Thursday into Friday \nmorning. This will allow for a slight warmup across the region \nbefore the models start hinting at the potential for another weak \nshortwave trough moving across the region Friday afternoon into \nSaturday.\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms in Southwest Montana Today: \nScattered showers and thunderstorms are possible this afternoon \nalong the Montana/Idaho border into central Madison and Gallatin \ncounties. Precipitation amounts are not expected to be high with \ngenerally a couple hundredths of an inch possible. Severe weather is \nnot expected with any of these thunderstorms with only gusts in the \n30-40 mph range expected.\n\n\nStrong Wind on Tuesday:\nThe highest confidence for high winds remains in the portion of the \nNorthern Rockies and Glacier National Park highlighted in the High \nWind Watch. Across the rest of the northern plains, there is upper-\nlevel support for high winds but no good mechanism to bring those \nwinds to the surface. The one exception might be Cut Bank which is \ndependent on where mountain wave activity sets up. But even so, \nconfidence is less than 50% across all zones which means that, for \nnow, no expansions will be made to the High Wind Watch.\n\n\nElevated Fire Weather Concerns on Tuesday:\nWith gusty winds, minimum RH values in the 20s, and highs in the 70s \nacross much of Central and North-Central Montana, there is a concern \nfor elevated fire weather conditions through the afternoon and \nevening hours. Conditions are not critical enough to put out any \nfire weather products but they are elevated enough that ongoing \nfires or new starts may be difficult to contain at times. Folks \nshould continue to take all preventative measures to reduce sparks.\n-thor",
